Answer: Change the classes into interfaces, and let one class implement both of them.

```java
interface Calculator1 {
void add(int a, int b);
}
interface Calculator2 {
void subtract(int a, int b);
}
class Demo implements Calculator1, Calculator2 {
public void add(int a, int b) {
System.out.println("Sum: " + (a + b));
}
public void subtract(int a, int b) {
System.out.println("Difference: " + (a - b));
}
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Demo d = new Demo();
d.add(10, 5);
d.subtract(10, 5);
}
}
```

Output: Sum: 15 Difference: 5 Explanation: Demo gets the contents of both interfaces, so multiple inheritance is achieved through interfaces and not through classes.

Answer: Yes. A variable inside an interface is public static final by default, just like the methods are public abstract by default. Java adds these modifiers even if you do not write them.

```java
interface Calculator {
int x = 10;      // same as: public static final int x = 10;
}
```

Answer: The variable is final, so it must be initialized when it is declared, and its value can never be changed. Trying to change it is an error.

```java
